  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1248 AM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe active weather pattern continues with increasing southwest flow \non Thursday ahead of the next western U.S. upper low advancing \neastward towards the Great Basin. Snow spreads into the \nsouthwest mountains and Continental Divide Thursday night \nthrough Friday as energy ejecting from the mean upper trough \nglances by to the northwest. Main upper trough ejects eastward \nacross CO on Saturday, though the system is pretty fast moving \nand open, suggesting colder temperatures and some brief \nrain/snow showers behind the frontal passage Friday \nnight/Saturday. Snow amounts across the mountains look light at \nthis point, as the system appears to lack a persistent deep \nmoisture tap. But the snow will be wind driven with the \npotential for 2-6 inches across the Eastern San Juan mountains \nthrough early Saturday morning where orographics will be\nmaximized. The central mountains will see considerably less \ngiven the less favorable orographic component. And the southeast\nmountains and plains will see a brief chance for showers but \ngiven the open and progressive nature of the system, any \namounts look localized and brief unless the upper circulation \ncan intensify a bit more and pull winds to a more northeasterly \nupslope component. NBM shows the odds of snow amounts greater \nthan an inch at less than 25% across the southeast mountains \nand Palmer Divide, so odds of measurable snow is not looking \ngood at this point. Will have to watch the potential for some \nstrong winds on Friday with the latest data suggesting gusts up\nto 50 to 60 mph will be possible.\n\nTemperatures will remain cool through early next week with another \nfast moving upper trough moving through Monday or Tuesday.  It \ndoesn't look like a big precipitation maker at this point, but \nmore light snow for the Continental Divide looks probable along\nwith cooler than normal temperatures for the plains. -KT",
